# Build context exclusions.
#
# All three Dockerfiles use the repository root as their context, so without
# this every build uploads node_modules, the Python virtualenv, test artefacts
# and the local data directory to the daemon -- hundreds of megabytes, on every
# build, to produce images that use none of it.
#
# It is also a security boundary. The context is what a Dockerfile *can* copy;
# anything excluded here cannot end up in a layer by accident. A stray `.env`
# or a copy of the invoice vault baked into a published image is not something
# a later fix can remove, because the layer is still there in the registry.

# --- Dependencies (reinstalled inside the image from lockfiles) -------------
**/node_modules
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# --- Secrets and local configuration ---------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/.aws
**/.npmrc

# --- Runtime data ----------------------------------------------------------
# The invoice vault holds original supplier PDFs and the JSON stores hold real
# booking and feedback history. Neither belongs in an image.
data/
!data/.gitkeep

# --- Build output (rebuilt in the image) -----------------------------------
**/dist
**/build
frontend/dist

# --- Tests and artefacts ---------------------------------------------------
**/coverage
e2e/
playwright-report/
test-results/
frontend/src/test/
backend/tests/
**/*.test.js
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
**/*.spec.ts
**/*.spec.tsx

# --- VCS and tooling -------------------------------------------------------
.git
.github
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea

# --- Docs ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Excluded from the context, not from the repository. Documentation changes on
# a different cadence than code, and including it would invalidate the layer
# cache for a typo fix in a README.
*.md
docs/
